{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1102869-what-to-tell-new-players","id":1102869,"title":"What to Tell New Players","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","World of Darkness","Mage: The Ascension"],"posts":[{"date":"2017-06-12T10:54:54-07:00","id":1102869,"title":"What to Tell New Players","content":"As a followup to my last <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1101620-m20-worth-it-for-my-game\">post</a> about whether or not to use M20, I'm trying to decide what my players need to read, and how much I should tell them. Most of my players are familiar with a version of the ST system (Ex3). Most are not familiar with even the lore of oWoD and definitely not Mage.<br /> <br /> Part of me thinks this is a great opportunity; they can make vanilla mortals and experience the World of Darkness IC without any OC baggage. On the other hand, I'm not sure what, if anything, I should have them read from the books before we start.<br /> <br /> I think the basic rule set is necessary for character creation, and I want them to be at least familiar with how Sphere magick works. Other than that, what, if anything, should I have them read before we sit down to play the game?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/6601-totentanz","name":"Totentanz","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar6601_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1293"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-06-12T11:29:33-07:00","id":1102882,"title":"","content":"My experience is that it's best to allow new players to create a character that is new to the supernatural world and allow them time to discover things as the game progresses naturally. When building characters we did it as a group session as a sort of &quot;session 0&quot; and I explained mechanics as necessary. They didn't choose their Convention (mine is a Technocracy game) until after we had solid concepts thought out and partially translated into dots on a sheet. They made a person first, and then chose the best faction that fit their character. It's ok if they don't understand the mechanics of the game very well because that's my job as ST. They'll pick it up over time anyway.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12710-pazuzuaxelf","name":"PazuzuAxelf","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"218"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-06-12T17:01:10-07:00","id":1102953,"title":"","content":"I would focus more on concepts than mechanics. If I recall from your other post, you're making your own Tradition/Craft/Group set up rather than using the standard setting. I would start with those. Let your players get an idea of the kind of Mage archetype they'd be most interested in playing, and worry about the mechanics later.<br /> <br /> A quick write up of sphere magick is pretty good, don't get me wrong, but I'd recommend focusing on the general idea at this stage. I've had pretty good luck describing spheres as building blocks for magic, and then giving the rough ideas of what each sphere means: i.e. &quot;Life has to do with living things, and includes healing and shapeshifting.&quot;<br /> <br /> Obviously, once they get to that part of the character creation stage, they'll want to look deeper into what they'll be able to actually do. But hopefully by then they'll have a rough idea of what they want to focus on, and therefore it'll be less overwhelming.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":2430,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2430-kalinara","user":"kalinara","date":". 06-12-2017, 08:05 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2430-kalinara","name":"kalinara","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2430_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"582"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-06-13T15:07:32-07:00","id":1103298,"title":"","content":"Personally, I think M20 is a terrible introduction to the spheres. Paradigm and craft may have been compartmentalised and explained rather well, but the spheres themselves are much harder to figure out than in Revised. Their basic effects thing assumes a level of familiarity with the system and that themes carries on for the whole chapter, and HDYDT does nothing to alliviate this problem. If anything it just makes things worse.<br /> <br /> If you want players to get a handle of the spheres, have then read Revised, once they've gotten to grips with that, try introducing them to M20's jibbering heap of vagueness.<br /> <br /> Either that or just play with Revised's spheres and add a few M20 things that you like, such as Spirit giving soak to Spirit attacks, or Life pattern locking, and Prime sense for auras.<br /> <br /> I see M20 as more of a toy box than a rule book.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/959615-chronicle-report-keepers-of-the-wyck\" target=\"_blank\">Keepers of the Wyck: A Chronicle I'm running</a> FINALE: Chapter 39: Green Fairy","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12193-saikou","name":"Saikou","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12193_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1406"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-06-22T01:37:10-07:00","id":1106446,"title":"","content":"I've just started a campaign exactly like this - players don't know the setting, and start as sleepers, awakening first session as a side effect of a disaster. <br /> All I asked them for was a loose concept, and we filled out the character sheets as the first session continued. <br /> I made a list of vague descriptions of the Traditions' paradigms, to give them something to have their characters believe that might help them fit into a tradition later. Would it help if I shared it with you? I've got a writeup of the first bit of the initial session with stages and reasons to fill in the character sheet as we go as well.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/13587-eyrelliah","name":"Eyrelliah","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar13587_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"92"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-06-22T01:50:24-07:00","id":1106447,"title":"","content":"Yeah, starting off with sleepers awakening and learning everything ingame is the way to go (imo).","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1235-eadee","name":"Eadee","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1235_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"209"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}